{0 CamlCast}

A first-person raycasting engine in OCaml on top of SDL2 ([tsdl]).

A {!Camlcast_core.World} is a graph of independently-authored
{!Camlcast_core.Room}s, each in its own coordinates. Rooms are joined at named
doorways that are both visible and walkable. An open doorway is a portal, drawn
recursively into the opening; a {!Camlcast_core.Transform} carries the camera
and the ray across.

Rooms are built from wall {e segments} at any angle, not cells of a grid. The
floor is an inclined {!Camlcast_core.Plane}. The ceiling, per room, is an
inclined plane or an open {!Camlcast_core.Sky}. Walls carry their own heights
and {!Camlcast_core.Material}s, can be see-through, and can be hung with decals.

{!Camlcast_core.Image} sprites stand in the world facing the player. Mouse look
with pitch is supported.

Each frame is drawn into a CPU pixel buffer. The floor, ceiling and sky are cast
per pixel. The opaque walls, with their decals, are painted over them. The
sprites and the see-through walls are then painted together, farthest first, so
a sprite can stand in front of a window and be seen through one behind it.

Surfaces take their patterns from a procedural {!Camlcast_core.Texture} and
their colour from their material, dimmed by the world's
{!Camlcast_core.Atmosphere}. The GPU then scales the buffer to the window.

{1 Three guides}

- {{!page-"making-a-game"} {b Making a game on CamlCast}} — from an empty
  directory to a game, one feature at a time, with the demo that isolates
  each one. Start here to build something on the engine.
- {{!page-"building-the-engine"} {b Building the engine from scratch}} — how the
  picture is drawn, rebuilt by hand in eight phases with every derivation
  written out. Start here to learn how a raycaster works.
- {{!page-"building-the-layer"} {b Building the layer from scratch}} — how a
  description of a world becomes one, and how a component keeps state across a
  frame that rebuilt everything: elements, reconciling, hooks on effect
  handlers, and the seam between the two. The second half of the one above.

{1 The libraries}

The engine holds no content: no colours, patterns, pictures or rooms. It
provides the types those things are values of. A game supplies its own content,
so two games can share an engine without sharing a look.

- {b [camlcast]} — {b the layer, and what a game opens.} The parts a world is
  described with, the components that hold its state, and the loop that plays
  one. Its interface is written by hand rather than generated, so it is also
  the reading order: {!Camlcast}.
- {b [camlcast.loom]} — the runtime under the layer: elements, reconciling,
  hooks, contexts, a store. It depends only on the standard library and
  contains no engine types. The four modules a game names are re-exported by
  {!Camlcast}; the rest of it is at {!Camlcast_loom}.
- {b [camlcast.core]} — the platform. Geometry, ray casting, rendering, SDL:
  {!Camlcast_core}. A game that genuinely needs {!Camlcast_core.World} or
  {!Camlcast_core.Renderer} adds this to its dune file, which makes bypassing
  the layer a decision rather than a drift.
- {b [camlcast-demo]} — the demos and the art they are made of, run by
  [camlcast-demo]. One small world per engine feature, plus the five-room
  showcase that has all of them at once. The demos are content and live outside
  the library they are content for; they stay in this repository because
  together they exercise every corner of the engine.

{1 Building a game on it}

A game hands over what its world should be, every frame, and never touches the
loop. {!Camlcast.Run.play} opens a window, plays a description on it until the
player quits, and closes it again. {!Camlcast.Run.on} is the same run on a
window already open. A launcher uses it so that going from its menu to a level
changes the picture without closing the window.

{[
  let () = ignore (Run.play ~title:"A game" level)
]}

A description is made with {!Camlcast.P} — [world], [room], [boundary],
[doorway], [sprite], [hud]. The parts that have to remember something are
{e components}: functions from props to a description. A component holds state
with {!Camlcast.Hook.use_state} and reaches the outside world through
{!Camlcast.Hook.use_effect}. {!Camlcast.Events} reads the frame being rendered:
how long the last one took, what the player is pressing, what the crosshair is
on, which doorways were crossed. {!Camlcast.Store} holds state that belongs to
no component in particular: a score, an inventory, which chapter the player is
in.

Everything the loop acts on by itself is one record, {!Camlcast.Controls.t}:
which controls walk and look, which leaves the run, which works whatever the
crosshair is on, and which shows the map over the world. Each is a list of
{!Camlcast_core.Input.control}s, so a mouse button and a key can do the same job
and an empty list turns something off.

The run reports how it ended, as a {!Camlcast.Run.ending}. [Closed] means the
window was shut. [Returned] means the run finished on its own terms: a leaving
control, or a description that said {!Camlcast.P.finish}. Only a program that
plays one run after another needs the difference.

{2 The platform underneath}

None of the above is where the loop actually is. {!Camlcast_core.Engine.run}
plays a state of whatever type a game likes, described by an
{!Camlcast_core.Engine.type-game}: [update], given the frame's length, the
movement asked for and what the player is holding; [view], which world and which
player to draw from; and optionally [overlay], [pointing], [finished] and
[bindings]. {!Camlcast_core.Engine.run_world} is that loop over a bare
{!Camlcast_core.World} and the player walking it, plus an optional
[extend : World.t -> Player.t -> World.t] called on any frame the player crossed
into another room on.

This is the API the layer stands on: {!Camlcast.Run} is one call to
{!Camlcast_core.Engine.run} with a mount kept beside it. A game reaches for it
when it genuinely needs a
{!Camlcast_core.World} or a {!Camlcast_core.Renderer}, by adding
[camlcast.core] to its dune file.

Input arrives as controls, not as actions: {!Camlcast_core.Input.pressed},
{!Camlcast_core.Input.released}, {!Camlcast_core.Input.val-down} and
{!Camlcast_core.Input.held_for} over a {!Camlcast_core.Key} or a mouse button.
What "interact" or "journal" mean is the game's own mapping from controls to
actions.

Walking works the same way. {!Camlcast_core.Binding} is the table a game hands
over. It covers the four movement axes, the key that goes fullscreen, and the
optional key that ends the run. {!Camlcast_core.Binding.default} is WASD, the
arrows and the mouse; it is a default, not a rule.

An axis adds up terms, and the two kinds of term are added differently. A held
control or a stick is a {e rate}: summed, clamped to [[-1, 1]] and paid out at
the axis's speed over the frame. The mouse is a {e displacement} that has
already moved and is added as it stands. {!Camlcast_core.Binding.motion} reads a
table without modifying anything; a second kind of device would be added there.

Time passes only while the window has focus.

{1 The house rules}

The API follows a few conventions, so knowing one module is knowing them all.

- {b Constructors are [make].} A value with one natural way in gets
  [Module.make]. Configuration is labelled; the subject of the call — a
  picture, a material, two endpoints — is positional at the end. Domain-named
  constructors stand beside [make] where the domain has better words:
  {!Camlcast_core.Color.rgb}, {!Camlcast_core.Plane.horizontal},
  {!Camlcast_core.Texture.generate}, {!Camlcast_core.Transform.between},
  {!Camlcast_core.Room.doorway}.
- {b All-optional constructors end in [()]}, and each names its defaults in
  its own doc: {!Camlcast_core.Atmosphere.make}, {!Camlcast_core.Sky.make},
  {!Camlcast_core.Binding.make}, {!Camlcast_core.Engine.val-game}. Where the
  default is a value worth having, it is also exported —
  {!Camlcast_core.Atmosphere.default}, {!Camlcast_core.Sky.default},
  {!Camlcast_core.Binding.default} — as the value to start from and perturb.
- {b Reading a file is [load]}, always [string -> (t, [ `Msg of string ])
  result]. Every fallible call in the library carries that same closed
  error: {!Camlcast_core.Bitmap.load}, {!Camlcast_core.Image.load},
  {!Camlcast_core.Texture.load}, and the [of_asset] pair that goes through
  {!Camlcast_core.Asset} first.
- {b Every number states its unit and range where it is declared.} Distances
  and heights are in cells; one cell equals one texture repeat, and the eye
  stands {!Camlcast_core.Config.eye_height} up. Angles are radians on
  {!Camlcast_core.Vec.of_angle}'s reckoning. Colour channels and alpha run
  0 .. 255; fades and fractions run [0. .. 1.]. A constructor checks anything
  it would divide by or fade across; refusals are written as the negation of
  what would pass, so a [nan] is refused along with the out-of-range.
- {b Changing an immutable value}: [with_x] hands back the same thing with one
  component replaced ({!Camlcast_core.Room.with_sprites},
  {!Camlcast_core.World.with_atmosphere} — and {!Camlcast_core.Room.with_door},
  whose "thing" is a single threshold); [add_x]
  appends ({!Camlcast_core.Room.add_decal}, {!Camlcast_core.World.add_room});
  [set_x] is an engine-mediated change that keeps a cross-cutting invariant
  ({!Camlcast_core.World.set_door}, which changes both sides of a link at once).
- {b The winding rule is stated once}, at the top of {!Camlcast_core.Room};
  everything else points there.

{1 Where to start}

{!Camlcast_core.Ray} carries the ray-versus-segment derivation and why the
distance it reports is free of fish-eye. {!Camlcast_core.Plane} carries the
one-line equation that casts the sloped floor and ceiling per pixel.
{!Camlcast_core.Viewport} ties them to the screen: the projection, the eye
height, the pitch shear, and the rules that decide what a resize does — the
window is resizable and can go fullscreen at any frame.
{!Camlcast_core.Renderer} puts it all together. {!Camlcast_core.World} carries
both the portal machinery and the three primitives a world grows by.

{!Camlcast_core} lists the whole platform, alphabetically. The modules are
layered — each depends only on the ones under it — which the listing does not
show and {{!page-"building-the-engine"} Building the engine from scratch}
builds them in.

{1 The demos}

[camlcast-demo] with no argument opens the list in a window; [--list] prints
it instead. [camlcast-demo <name>] runs one. Each is a single file containing
only the feature it demonstrates. They are the other package, so their pages
are its own: {{:../camlcast-demo/index.html} the demos}.
